1
00:00:01,840 --> 00:00:06,610
你的脑内, 仍是一个尚未开发的世界。
Inside your head is an unexplored world.

2
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:13,050
里面储存着所有你知道的、你所有的感觉、
It holds all that you know, everything you feel,

3
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:16,430
以及你从未意识到的想法。
and thoughts you aren't even aware of.

4
00:00:17,030 --> 00:00:21,150
现代科学正在寻找隐藏于我们思想后的东西，
Now science is searching the hidden reaches of our minds,

5
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:24,420
解析其内在语言
decoding its inner language

6
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:27,390
来发现我们真正的精神力量
to discover our true mental power

7
00:00:27,510 --> 00:00:33,210
了解潜意识的秘密。
and to understand the mysteries of the subconscious.

8
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:41,690
《同摩根·弗里曼一起穿越虫洞》
空间、时间、生命本身
Space, time, life itself.

9
00:00:43,890 --> 00:00:48,180
宇宙的秘密，尽在穿越虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie through the wormhole.

10
00:00:53,590 --> 00:00:58,180
潜意识

11
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:03,840
潜意识--
The subconscious --

12
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:09,680
是我们害怕和期望的源头，
it's the source of our primal fears and desires,

13
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:12,720
也是我们梦境之源，
the wellspring of our dreams,

14
00:01:12,830 --> 00:01:16,720
是我们不同的‘自我’所隐藏的地方。
the place where our alter egos lurk.

15
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:18,860
但对于现代神经科学来说
But as modern neuroscience

16
00:01:18,970 --> 00:01:21,960
探索我们思维的边缘，
explores the frontiers of our minds,

17
00:01:22,070 --> 00:01:23,330
让我们发现
we're discovering

18
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:27,000
潜意识在清醒的每时每刻都在控制着我们。
the subconscious controls our every waking moment.

19
00:01:27,110 --> 00:01:31,600
若能真正了解我们认为没头绪的思维，
If we can truly understand our unthinking minds,

20
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:35,480
我们可能会变得比希望的更聪明、更健康、
we could all become smarter, healthier,

21
00:01:35,590 --> 00:01:40,790
和更具创造性。
and more creative than we ever dreamed possible.

22
00:01:43,630 --> 00:01:47,520
8岁的时候，我第一次上台表演。
I was eight years old when I got on stage for the first time.

23
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:51,120
我反复出演“忧郁男孩”。
I had to re-enact "Little Boy Blue."

24
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:55,360
尽管台词只是12句长的诗，
Even though the poem was only 12 lines long,

25
00:01:55,470 --> 00:01:58,240
但我总觉得我会出问题，
I was certain I was going to mess it up.

26
00:02:00,380 --> 00:02:02,940
当我准备开口时，
But the moment I began to speak,

27
00:02:03,050 --> 00:02:06,780
一部分隐藏的我似乎要接管我的大脑。
some hidden part of me seemed to take over.

28
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:14,250
我流畅毫无障碍的说出台词。
The lines flowed out of me without a hitch.

29
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:18,060
我感觉自己似乎不在这儿。
And I felt like I wasn't even there.

30
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:23,690
我们如何做出这么复杂的事情
How is it we are able to do something so complex

31
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:25,900
甚至无需思考？
without even thinking?

32
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:33,130
Marcus Raichle是位神经科学家
Marcus Raichle is a neuroscientist

33
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:35,970
就职于华盛顿大学医学院
at Washington University School of Medicine

34
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:37,770
在圣路易斯，密苏里州。
in St. Louis, Missouri.

35
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:44,080
他对于大脑的功能始终感到惊奇。
He is constantly amazed at what our brains can do.

36
00:02:44,190 --> 00:02:45,340
我们拥有记忆
We have memories

37
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:47,250
这些记忆使我知道如何移动我的手和脚
that have to do with how I move my hands and legs

38
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:48,950
或者其他这类事，
and all this kind of thing,

39
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:51,320
而我们自己却毫无意识
for which we have no conscious sense

40
00:02:51,430 --> 00:02:53,650
不知道这些如何完成的。
of how this is all implemented.

41
00:02:53,770 --> 00:02:57,830
我们似乎并不记得这些事的复杂程度。
We kind of forget how complicated these things are.

42
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:02,090
作为一名业余音乐家，
As an amateur musician,

43
00:03:02,210 --> 00:03:05,530
Marcus喜欢把大脑中不同功能的部分进行比较，
Marcus likes to compare our brain's separate functions,

44
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:09,770
比如视觉、听觉、记忆和肌肉控制，
like vision, hearing, memory, and muscle control,

45
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:13,270
就像一场音乐合奏中的每个演奏者。
to the individual players in a musical ensemble.

46
00:03:13,390 --> 00:03:14,770
对于他来说，
And for him,

47
00:03:14,890 --> 00:03:18,950
大脑的奇迹就是如何将这些复杂的系统
the miracle of the brain is how it gets these complex systems

48
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:22,390
调整到完美和谐...
to perform in perfect harmony...

49
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:36,870
...而不是像这样...
...rather than like this...

50
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:49,780
经过多年的大脑成像研究，
After years spent imaging brains,

51
00:03:49,890 --> 00:03:54,210
Marcus终于发现了一个完整的精神网络
Marcus eventually discovered an entire mental network

52
00:03:54,330 --> 00:03:57,450
可以对我们的行为与感觉进行调整，
that coordinates our movements with our senses,

53
00:03:57,560 --> 00:04:01,950
当我们停止思考时，它就开始发挥作用。
and it turns on the moment you stop thinking.

54
00:04:02,070 --> 00:04:04,220
从没人发现过这个网络。
Nobody was even looking for this.

55
00:04:04,340 --> 00:04:06,290
我们基本上出于偶然。
It was almost by accident.

56
00:04:06,410 --> 00:04:07,430
它引起了我们的注意
It came to our attention

57
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:09,360
基本就是你躺在扫描仪中
that if you just were laying in a scanner

58
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:11,000
我们看着你的大脑
and we were looking at your brain

59
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:13,230
然后我们让你做些事，
and then we asked you to do something,

60
00:04:13,350 --> 00:04:17,470
不仅做些向上的事，也有某些向下的。
【实在翻译无力啊...说的是什么呢...】
not only did things go up, but certain things went down.

61
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:22,210
大脑的一些特定区域，每次在我们开始检测时
Certain parts of the brain seem to turn off

62
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:24,540
都好像关闭了。
whenever we begin a task.

63
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:28,210
而当我们安静放松后，
Those same regions become highly active

64
00:04:28,330 --> 00:04:31,520
这些区域开始变得高度活跃，
whenever we are quiet and relaxed.

65
00:04:31,630 --> 00:04:35,860
Marcus称这些相互联系的脑部区域为
Marcus calls these linked sections of the brain

66
00:04:35,970 --> 00:04:38,790
“默认模式网络 ”，
the Default Mode Network,

67
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:41,730
这是因为我们什么事都不做的时候，
because the brain defaults to this activity

68
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:44,960
大脑就默认进入这种活跃状态。
whenever we are not doing anything else.

69
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:48,830
事实上，大脑的这种默认模式
In fact, the brain is just as active in this default mode

70
00:04:48,950 --> 00:04:51,840
就算我们有意识做别的事时，也会维持在活跃状态。
as when we're consciously doing something.

71
00:04:51,950 --> 00:04:57,380
经过仔细分析，Marcus认为他明白了为什么会这样。
After careful analysis, Marcus thinks he understands why.

72
00:04:57,490 --> 00:04:59,450
这种默认模式对于
The default mode is deeply important

73
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:01,180
创造一种预测接下来会发生什么
in creating an ability

74
00:05:01,290 --> 00:05:02,850
的能力非常重要。
to predict what's gonna happen next.

75
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:05,220
我认为这个真的非常重要。
I think it's really critical.

76
00:05:05,330 --> 00:05:09,590
就像音乐会的指挥
In the same way that an ensemble's musical director

77
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:11,660
组织各部分的音乐，
organizes the group's music,

78
00:05:11,770 --> 00:05:13,960
默认模式网络
the Default Mode Network

79
00:05:14,070 --> 00:05:17,130
同样组织大脑的全部功能
organizes all the functions of the brain

80
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:22,070
这样思维和身体就可以保持在“同一页”。
so that mind and body are always on the same page.

81
00:05:22,180 --> 00:05:24,270
但是，我们的潜意识始终是
But is our subconscious always the servant

82
00:05:24,380 --> 00:05:26,140
有意识思维的仆人么？
of our conscious mind?

83
00:05:26,250 --> 00:05:29,320
难道潜意识可以支配行为？
Or can it also take charge?

84
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:37,480
Henrik Ehrsson，是瑞典斯德哥尔摩的神经科学家，
Henrik Ehrsson, a neuroscientist in Stockholm, Sweden,

85
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:40,350
他对躯体非常着迷。
is obsessed with his body.

86
00:05:40,470 --> 00:05:44,590
事实上，他认为身体会不停的工作，
In fact, he thinks about bodies nonstop

87
00:05:44,700 --> 00:05:47,160
他想知道
because he wants to know

88
00:05:47,270 --> 00:05:51,260
我们的思维到底如何控制身体。
exactly how our minds control our bodies.

89
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:53,100
我站的这个地方，
Well, here I stand,

90
00:05:53,210 --> 00:05:57,170
和雕塑一样有皮肤、骨头和关节。
like a sculpture of skin and bone and joints.

91
00:05:57,280 --> 00:05:58,640
但是我感觉我活着。
But I feel alive.

92
00:05:58,750 --> 00:06:02,540
我能感到意识贯穿了我的整个身体。
I feel my conscious experience in my entire body.

93
00:06:02,650 --> 00:06:05,020
我的身体就是我的一部分。
My body feels like part of me.

94
00:06:06,590 --> 00:06:10,520
我感兴趣的是想要了解为什么会这样，
And what I'm interested in is to understand how can that can be.

95
00:06:10,630 --> 00:06:12,250
我们如何感受到身体
How can we have this experience

96
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:13,990
是我们自己的一部分，
of our own body as part of ourselves,

97
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:16,600
而不同于这个世界的其他结构？
distinct from the rest of the world?

98
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:23,670
欢迎来到玩偶屋。
Welcome to the Dollhouse.

99
00:06:24,810 --> 00:06:26,530
这些残肢、摄像机和刀
These limbs, cameras, and knives

100
00:06:26,650 --> 00:06:29,740
只是一个科学实验的一部分
are actually part of a scientific experiment

101
00:06:29,850 --> 00:06:31,140
这个实验由Henrik设计
designed by Henrik

102
00:06:31,250 --> 00:06:34,510
来测试我们的潜意识感觉，
to play with our subconscious mind's sense

103
00:06:34,620 --> 00:06:36,440
比如我们是谁，我们在哪儿。
of who and where we are.

104
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:39,180
我们知道大脑中有很多进程
Well, we knew that there's lots of processes in the brain

105
00:06:39,290 --> 00:06:42,150
一般都处于潜意识水平而我们无法意识到。
that we are not aware of that happens at a subconscious level.

106
00:06:42,260 --> 00:06:45,850
我们想知道的是，是什么使这些信号
What we try to understand is what makes those signals

107
00:06:45,960 --> 00:06:48,870
成为我们意识体验的一部分。
become part of our conscious experience.

108
00:06:57,510 --> 00:07:00,970
我们让受试者进入房间，
A blindfolded subject is led into a room

109
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:02,770
屋子里有两张床，
with two beds,

110
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:06,010
一张是他的，另一张上面是个小的人体模型。
one for himself and the other for a small doll.

111
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:08,870
整个实验中你不要动，这是非常重要的。
It's important that you try not to move during the experiment.

112
00:07:08,990 --> 00:07:10,780
好吗？
Okay?

113
00:07:10,890 --> 00:07:14,180
我们使用头戴式显示器，与摄像机相连。
We use head-mounted displays that we connect to cameras.

114
00:07:14,290 --> 00:07:18,750
这样，受试者眼前就有两个屏幕。
So, there's two screens in front of the participant eyes.

115
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:21,450
每个屏幕与一个摄像机联接，
And each screen is connected to one video camera,

116
00:07:21,570 --> 00:07:25,590
我们就可以从这个装置中看到人体模型，
which we can mount on the head of a mannequin or a doll.

117
00:07:25,700 --> 00:07:28,390
如果你往下看，是看不到自己身体的。
So when you look down, you don't see your own body anymore.

118
00:07:28,510 --> 00:07:29,530
你看到的是人体模型，
You see the doll

119
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:34,100
而且是从很自然的第一人称视角。
from the natural, first-person perspective.

120
00:07:34,210 --> 00:07:38,070
受试者可以感受到有东西触摸自己的腿，
The subject feels the stroking on his leg

121
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:42,070
但他只能看到玩偶的腿正被触摸的图像，
but sees the stroking on the doll's leg,

122
00:07:42,190 --> 00:07:44,940
这样就欺骗了他的大脑，
so his brain is fooled into thinking

123
00:07:45,060 --> 00:07:48,650
认为玩偶的腿就是自己的腿。
that the doll's legs are actually his own.

124
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:50,780
接下来发生的
And what happens then

125
00:07:50,900 --> 00:07:53,990
是大脑混淆了你看到的和你感觉到的，
is the brain just fuses what you see and what you feel,

126
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:56,920
很快，你感觉自己就像玩偶或人形模特。
and boom, you feel like the doll or the mannequin.

127
00:07:57,040 --> 00:07:59,990
我们认为大脑创造了
We think the brain creates

128
00:08:00,110 --> 00:08:03,660
自己身体的内模，
like an internal model of your own body,

129
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:05,660
大脑完成这些是通过
and we think the brain does that

130
00:08:05,780 --> 00:08:09,440
整合所有感觉上的可用信息实现的
by integrating all available information from all the senses

131
00:08:09,550 --> 00:08:11,740
并以此来判断
and be part of making that decision

132
00:08:11,850 --> 00:08:13,510
“这是我”或“这不是我”。
that "this is me" or "this is not me."

133
00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:17,810
通过使大脑进入虚假现实，
Having tricked the brain into a false reality,

134
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:20,350
Henrik现在可以扭曲事实
Henrik can now tweak that reality

135
00:08:20,460 --> 00:08:26,420
来显示潜意识的力量究竟有多大。
and reveal how powerful the subconscious actually is.

136
00:08:26,530 --> 00:08:29,690
研究者用刀威胁玩偶。
The researcher threatens the doll with the knife.

137
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:32,690
受试者害怕得躲避。
The subject flinches with horror.

138
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:37,960
他的大脑不禁认为自身将要经历痛苦。
His brain can't help but expect excruciating pain.

139
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:41,370
甚至受试者之后已经知道这不是真的，
Even after the subject realizes it was a trick,

140
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:43,940
当重复这一试验时
he continues to have the same reaction

141
00:08:44,050 --> 00:08:45,800
他对此仍有相同的反应。
when the illusion is repeated.

142
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:48,270
他的意识体验
His conscious experience

143
00:08:48,390 --> 00:08:51,840
仍无法压倒潜意识反应。
cannot override his subconscious reaction.

144
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:54,050
这在你脑中挥之不去，
You can't think it away.

145
00:08:54,160 --> 00:08:56,650
你知道这仅仅是个实验。
You know it's just, you know, an experiment.

146
00:08:56,760 --> 00:08:59,950
但你的身体不禁感到 “啊！”
But you can't help that bodily feeling of "ugh"

147
00:09:00,070 --> 00:09:01,990
因为你认为这个玩偶就是你，
because you feel that this doll is you,

148
00:09:02,100 --> 00:09:05,660
这样在一种基础水平上，你的大脑就会开始反应，
so your brain just reacts in a very sort of basic way,

149
00:09:05,770 --> 00:09:08,660
那个信号，就是这个反应，也就是我们正在测量的
and that signal, this reaction, is what we're measuring

150
00:09:08,770 --> 00:09:11,170
确实证实了那个幻像像是真的。
to really prove that the illusion is real.

151
00:09:14,610 --> 00:09:16,540
Henrik的这些能让人
One of Henrik's goals

152
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:19,710
感到害怕的视觉幻象实验一个目的
in performing these unnerving visual illusions

153
00:09:19,820 --> 00:09:23,640
就是精确定位潜意识大脑中
is to locate the precise part of the subconscious brain

154
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:26,590
让你感受自己身体的部分。
that keeps track of where your body is.

155
00:09:27,660 --> 00:09:30,480
在这种身体交换过程中对他进行的脑部扫描
Brain scans he's performed during a body swap

156
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:34,850
表现出腹外侧运动前皮质活性的增加，
point to increased activity in the ventral premotor cortex,

157
00:09:34,970 --> 00:09:39,630
这部分是涉及到视觉引导的运动的感觉神经元。
the sensory neurons involved in the visual guidance of movement.

158
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:41,290
那么这个腹外侧运动前皮质
Well, the ventral premotor cortex

159
00:09:41,410 --> 00:09:43,430
是大脑整合你的感觉和视觉的
is one of those key nodes in the brain

160
00:09:43,540 --> 00:09:45,500
重要神经节之一
that integrate what you feel and what you see

161
00:09:45,610 --> 00:09:47,810
可以以此控制身体、防御身体。
for controlling the body, for defending the body.

162
00:09:49,510 --> 00:09:50,700
如果你在打一场拳击，
Because if you're in a fight,

163
00:09:50,820 --> 00:09:52,910
你需要知道，你的身体在哪
you need to know, you know, where is your body

164
00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:55,510
你的敌人在哪，这样来采取行动。
and where is your opponent, and you need to be able to act.

165
00:09:55,620 --> 00:09:58,110
这样大脑中的那些回路
So those circuits of the brain

166
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:01,810
可以防止身体不受到威胁，
are built to defend the body from threat,

167
00:10:01,930 --> 00:10:03,920
通过联系环境中的物体
for interacting with objects in the world,

168
00:10:04,030 --> 00:10:05,080
使你总能维持
and always keep track

169
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:07,120
你和环境之间的界线。
of the boundary between you and the world.

170
00:10:07,230 --> 00:10:08,620
哦！
Ohh!

171
00:10:08,730 --> 00:10:15,030
知道哪些是我们哪些不是
Knowing the difference between what is us and not us

172
00:10:15,140 --> 00:10:16,600
是如此重要
is so vital

173
00:10:16,710 --> 00:10:18,860
以至于潜意识反应
that our subconscious reactions

174
00:10:18,980 --> 00:10:21,800
可以超越于我们的意识思维。
will overrule our conscious thoughts.

175
00:10:21,910 --> 00:10:23,640
有时这就意味着
And sometimes that can mean

176
00:10:23,750 --> 00:10:27,080
生与死的界限。
the difference between life and death.

177
00:10:30,970 --> 00:10:32,260
你是否有过一种预感，
Have you ever had a hunch,

178
00:10:32,380 --> 00:10:37,280
一种感觉有些是不对头的感觉？
a feeling that something was wrong?

179
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:40,500
我们很多人都有过。
Most of us have.

180
00:10:40,620 --> 00:10:43,910
但是这种感觉来自哪里？
But where does that feeling come from?

181
00:10:44,020 --> 00:10:48,140
这可能是你的潜意识传达的信息，
It could be a message from your subconscious mind,

182
00:10:48,260 --> 00:10:54,060
告诉你它知道你的意识所不知道的事。
telling you it knows something that you don't.

183
00:10:58,630 --> 00:11:02,630
Joshua Brown喜欢户外运动，
Joshua Brown is always on the go

184
00:11:02,740 --> 00:11:07,960
而且可以提前意识到意外。
and always anticipating the unexpected.

185
00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:10,300
我骑自行车出门，
When I go out cycling,

186
00:11:10,410 --> 00:11:12,900
到处都有潜在的危险。
there are dangers potentially everywhere.

187
00:11:13,020 --> 00:11:15,670
那个行人会突然跳到我面前么？
Is that pedestrian gonna jump in front of me?

188
00:11:15,780 --> 00:11:18,210
我前面那辆车的车门是否会突然打开？
Is that car door gonna open in front of me?

189
00:11:18,320 --> 00:11:20,580
那辆车会撞到我么？
Is that car gonna cut me off?

190
00:11:20,690 --> 00:11:22,140
我就在不断的评估，
And I'm constantly evaluating,

191
00:11:22,260 --> 00:11:24,810
“是否有一些需要我注意的危险，
"is there some danger that I need to look out for,

192
00:11:24,930 --> 00:11:26,850
我需要小心避免这些危险的发生？”
that I need to be careful to avoid?"

193
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:29,520
不过Joshua在在布卢明顿的
But Joshua's research at Indiana University

194
00:11:29,630 --> 00:11:30,720
印第安纳大学的研究
in Bloomington

195
00:11:30,830 --> 00:11:33,590
表明这种意识评估
has shown him that this conscious evaluation

196
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:35,420
仅是我们大脑
is only the first layer

197
00:11:35,540 --> 00:11:38,190
危险感觉机制的第一层。
of our brain's danger-sensing mechanism.

198
00:11:38,310 --> 00:11:43,440
真正让我们避免伤害的是潜意识。
What really keeps us out of harm's way is our subconscious.

199
00:11:47,950 --> 00:11:50,170
Joshua发现一种方法
Joshua has found a way

200
00:11:50,290 --> 00:11:53,680
可以研究我们潜意识如何让我们预感到
of studying how our subconscious mind triggers a gut feeling

201
00:11:53,790 --> 00:11:56,380
会发生一些不好的事。
when something is about to go wrong.

202
00:11:56,490 --> 00:11:57,880
比如避开车流高峰时期
And it's a lot safer

203
00:11:57,990 --> 00:12:01,280
骑车就会安全得多。
than riding your bike through rush-hour traffic.

204
00:12:01,400 --> 00:12:02,420
基本上，每个实验组，
Basically, on each trial,

205
00:12:02,530 --> 00:12:04,290
都会在屏幕中间
there's gonna be an arrow that appears

206
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:05,490
出现一个箭头，
in the middle of the screen,

207
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:07,860
箭头会指向左或是右。
and the arrow points left or right.

208
00:12:07,970 --> 00:12:09,430
这非常简单。
And it's really simple.

209
00:12:09,540 --> 00:12:11,290
箭头指向左，就按左键
Press the left button if the arrow points left

210
00:12:11,410 --> 00:12:13,570
箭头指向右，就按右键。
and press the right button if the arrow points right.

211
00:12:14,980 --> 00:12:16,630
但会有陷阱。
But there is a catch.

212
00:12:16,750 --> 00:12:22,610
有时半秒内，会出现另一个反方向的箭头。
Sometimes, within a half second, an opposing arrow appears.

213
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:26,540
他的按键就必须按照这个箭头的方向。
And he must press that arrow's direction instead.

214
00:12:26,660 --> 00:12:30,880
每次实验只持续一秒，
Each trial only lasts one second,

215
00:12:30,990 --> 00:12:36,090
那么如果他太快做决定，就会错过第二个箭头。
so if he decides too quickly, he might miss the second arrow.

216
00:12:36,200 --> 00:12:39,900
太慢，就会没时间。
Too slowly and he could run out of time.

217
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:43,590
那么我们等待的越久，
And so the longer we wait,

218
00:12:43,710 --> 00:12:45,260
就会感觉越来不及
the more likely it is it's too late

219
00:12:45,370 --> 00:12:47,340
他们就按了错误的按钮。
and they've already pressed the wrong button.

220
00:12:50,010 --> 00:12:52,640
Joshua在测试中加入了一种模式，
Joshua has programmed a pattern into the test,

221
00:12:52,750 --> 00:12:57,070
但这种模式很微妙，受试者无法通过意识识别。
but it's too subtle for the subject to pick up consciously.

222
00:12:57,190 --> 00:13:02,810
这样，他就必须依赖于自己的预感。
Instead, he must rely on his gut feelings.

223
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:06,080
其实我们想做的是分离出大脑
What we really want to do is to isolate mechanisms in the brain

224
00:13:06,190 --> 00:13:08,880
当你觉得你更会犯错时
that might be able to signal

225
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,150
发出信号的机制。
when you think you're more likely to make a mistake.

226
00:13:12,270 --> 00:13:14,960
Joshua现在开始做同一个实验
Joshua now conducts the same experiment

227
00:13:15,070 --> 00:13:17,890
只是受试者在MRI（核磁共振成像）仪中。
while the subject is an MRI machine.

228
00:13:18,010 --> 00:13:22,260
他发现当受试者感觉要犯错时
He sees that one brain area in particular buzzes with activity

229
00:13:22,380 --> 00:13:26,740
大脑有一个区域活性增强。
when the subject feels like he might be about to make an error.

230
00:13:26,850 --> 00:13:30,650
这就是前扣带皮层。
It's the anterior cingulate cortex.

231
00:13:31,950 --> 00:13:34,680
你可以看这里，红色的，
And so, here you can see, in red,

232
00:13:34,790 --> 00:13:36,410
就是前扣带皮层
the anterior cingulate cortex

233
00:13:36,530 --> 00:13:39,280
正表现出强烈的错误可能。
is showing a strong effect of error likelihood.

234
00:13:39,390 --> 00:13:42,790
这样，就算受试者没有犯错，
That is, even when subjects don't actually make a mistake,

235
00:13:42,900 --> 00:13:44,750
这部分区域仍表现出
they're still showing effects in this region

236
00:13:44,870 --> 00:13:46,460
似乎会有
that seem to anticipate

237
00:13:46,570 --> 00:13:49,630
更大的错误可能性的效果。
the greater likelihood of making a mistake.

238
00:13:49,740 --> 00:13:54,800
Joshua认为前扣带皮质的这种活性
Joshua believes this activity in the anterior cingulate cortex

239
00:13:54,910 --> 00:13:57,430
是我们预感的源头。
is the source of our gut feelings.

240
00:13:57,550 --> 00:14:01,770
不过这不仅保护骑自行车穿越忙碌街道的我们。
But it's not just protecting us as we bike down busy streets.

241
00:14:01,880 --> 00:14:04,010
有时这种感觉会对
Sometimes it can be responsible

242
00:14:04,120 --> 00:14:06,420
上百条生命负责。
for the fate of hundreds of lives.

243
00:14:09,290 --> 00:14:14,080
1991年，海湾战争的开放日中
In 1991, in the opening days of the Persian Gulf War,

244
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:16,850
一个英国战舰舰队
a fleet of British battleships

245
00:14:16,970 --> 00:14:19,890
驻守在离科威特海岸20里远的地方。
is stationed 20 miles off the shores of Kuwait.

246
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,890
一个海军军官正独自监视雷达站
A lone naval officer is Manning the radar station

247
00:14:24,010 --> 00:14:27,860
这个雷达站可以预报正在来袭的威胁。
of one of the ships looking for incoming threats.

248
00:14:27,980 --> 00:14:29,300
一切都很平静
All is quiet

249
00:14:29,410 --> 00:14:32,500
直到一个光点突然出现在雷达屏幕上。
until a blip suddenly appears on the radar screen.

250
00:14:32,610 --> 00:14:35,670
这个光点只可能是两件事。
That radar blip could've been one of two things.

251
00:14:35,780 --> 00:14:38,170
或者是一架从轰炸航路
It could've been a friendly aircraft

252
00:14:38,290 --> 00:14:40,040
正在返航的友机，
returning from a bombing run,

253
00:14:40,160 --> 00:14:42,910
或者是伊拉克发射的
or it could've been a Silkworm missile

254
00:14:43,020 --> 00:14:44,910
蚕式导弹。
fired by the Iraqis.

255
00:14:45,030 --> 00:14:47,320
这个雷达军官需要做一个决定
The radar officer had a decision to make.

256
00:14:47,430 --> 00:14:50,890
他可以命令对其开火，
He could order the firing of countermeasures,

257
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,360
但如果他这么做了，
but if he did that, and it was a friendly aircraft,

258
00:14:53,470 --> 00:14:55,590
他就可能击毁一架友军飞机。
he would down a friendly aircraft.

259
00:14:55,700 --> 00:15:00,830
那么如果这个物体是一支敌人的导弹，
Now, if that incoming object was an enemy missile,

260
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:02,460
这条船上的几百人
several hundred people, along with the ship,

261
00:15:02,580 --> 00:15:03,600
就会在攻击中死亡。
could lose their lives.

262
00:15:03,710 --> 00:15:06,570
但他只有不到一分钟的时间做决定。
And he had less than a minute to decide.

263
00:15:06,680 --> 00:15:08,100
时间正在流逝
Time is running out.

264
00:15:08,220 --> 00:15:11,450
这个军官要做的完全凭感觉
But all the officer has to go on is a gut feeling.

265
00:15:12,790 --> 00:15:16,720
他命令发射导弹。
So he orders the launch of countermeasures.

266
00:15:21,330 --> 00:15:24,090
他看着雷达屏幕上的
He sees the still unidentified object

267
00:15:24,200 --> 00:15:26,720
未知物体消失。
fall off the radar screen.

268
00:15:26,840 --> 00:15:29,660
导弹摧毁了目标
The countermeasures destroy the target

269
00:15:29,770 --> 00:15:33,170
仅仅距离舰队不足半里的距离。
less than half a mile from the fleet of ships.

270
00:15:34,780 --> 00:15:36,870
他回到自己的房间里
He went back to his room and just --

271
00:15:36,980 --> 00:15:39,130
他为自己刚做的事纠结不已。
he was just tormented over what had happened.

272
00:15:39,250 --> 00:15:42,370
不过这种纠结很快就结束了，
His torment is short-lived.

273
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:44,110
雷达确认
Radio calls confirm

274
00:15:44,220 --> 00:15:48,040
这个目标的确是一枚蚕式导弹，
that the target was indeed a Silkworm missile,

275
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:50,150
并不是友军飞机。
not a friendly aircraft.

276
00:15:50,260 --> 00:15:53,450
这次事件后
In the final review of the incident,

277
00:15:53,560 --> 00:15:56,520
调查者发现有架友军飞机
investigators discovered that a friendly aircraft

278
00:15:56,630 --> 00:16:00,260
在第一次扫描时可能出现在雷达上，
would have appeared on the radar screen during the first sweep,

279
00:16:00,370 --> 00:16:03,930
而蚕式导弹一开始可能被
whereas the Silkworm missile would initially be masked

280
00:16:04,040 --> 00:16:05,460
地面干扰掩盖了。
by ground interference.

281
00:16:05,570 --> 00:16:08,000
直到雷达的第三次扫描才将导弹显示在屏幕上
So, it would only appear on the screen

282
00:16:08,110 --> 00:16:12,330
这时导弹已经距离舰队非常近了。
during the third radar sweep, as it got closer to the ships.

283
00:16:12,450 --> 00:16:13,700
这位军官的大脑
The officer's brain

284
00:16:13,820 --> 00:16:17,010
以某种方式发现了两种信号的不同，
had somehow picked up on this different pattern,

285
00:16:17,120 --> 00:16:19,980
尽管这在相关手册中都没有提到
even though it was not written in any manual

286
00:16:20,090 --> 00:16:24,650
而且他的主观意识也没有发现什么不同。
and he was not consciously aware of the difference.

287
00:16:24,760 --> 00:16:28,250
前扣带皮层基于遇敌的经验累积
The anterior cingulate cortex registered the danger

288
00:16:28,360 --> 00:16:32,690
表示这是个危险--
based on the experience of cumulative encounters --

289
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:35,960
什么看起来像是友军飞机，而什么看起来不像。
what looks like a friendly aircraft and what doesn't.

290
00:16:36,070 --> 00:16:38,690
这样他在几秒内的行为，
And so, by his action, in a matter of seconds,

291
00:16:38,810 --> 00:16:41,130
挽救了船只和上百条生命。
he saved the ship and hundreds of lives.

292
00:16:41,240 --> 00:16:44,700
大脑在不断的处理
The brain is constantly processing

293
00:16:44,810 --> 00:16:47,000
这类信号。
these kinds of signals.

294
00:16:47,120 --> 00:16:50,140
人们可能认为他们现在清醒
People may think that they've just now become aware

295
00:16:50,250 --> 00:16:51,840
或是刚做了决定，
or they've just now decided,

296
00:16:51,950 --> 00:16:53,810
但事实上，基本上
but in reality, most likely,

297
00:16:53,920 --> 00:16:57,710
大脑早就完成了对这些信息的处理。
their brain was processing that information well before.

298
00:16:57,830 --> 00:17:02,320
我们的潜意识一般都比我们快两步，
Our subconscious mind is always two steps ahead of us,

299
00:17:02,430 --> 00:17:06,590
甚至在我们还没意识到的情况下发出决定做什么的信号。
signaling what to do before we are even aware of it.

300
00:17:06,700 --> 00:17:11,200
那么其他人的大脑能察觉这些信号么？
Could other people's brains pick up these signals, too?

301
00:17:14,770 --> 00:17:16,490
肢体语言--
Body language --

302
00:17:17,810 --> 00:17:21,710
是潜意识的语言。
it's the native tongue of the subconscious.

303
00:17:22,140 --> 00:17:25,950
我们的行为方式及说话声调--
The way we move, the tone of our voice --

304
00:17:26,450 --> 00:17:30,270
不断透露着这些微妙的信号，
we are constantly giving off these subtle signals,

305
00:17:30,380 --> 00:17:34,680
这些信号对于我们来说意义深远。
and they can have a profound effect on those around us.

306
00:17:34,790 --> 00:17:38,810
你如何解码这种语言？
What if you could decode this language?

307
00:17:38,930 --> 00:17:43,260
我们实际上对别人说的是什么？
What are we really saying to one another?

308
00:17:43,930 --> 00:17:47,390
人类一直都在交流--
Humans are always communicating --

309
00:17:47,500 --> 00:17:49,630
聊天、通电话、
chatting, talking on the phone,

310
00:17:49,740 --> 00:17:53,060
发邮件、发Twitter和发短信。
sending e-mails, tweeting, and texting.

311
00:17:53,180 --> 00:17:57,770
麻省理工人类动力学实验室的“Sandy”Pentland
"Sandy" Pentland of M.I.T.'s Human Dynamics Lab

312
00:17:57,880 --> 00:18:00,670
试图从这些喧嚣中开始研究。
tries to rise above all this hubbub.

313
00:18:00,780 --> 00:18:04,110
他从另一角度研究我们如何交流。
He prefers a different perspective on how we connect.

314
00:18:04,220 --> 00:18:08,140
对于他来说，词语并不重要。
For him, words are not important.

315
00:18:08,260 --> 00:18:12,780
他关心的是交流的更原始的形式。
He cares about a more primal form of communication.

316
00:18:12,900 --> 00:18:16,220
他称之为“诚实信号”。
He calls it honest signaling.

317
00:18:16,330 --> 00:18:18,490
在我们拥有很多语言之前的群体
Groups, before we had much language,

318
00:18:18,600 --> 00:18:21,320
都共用一种语言(或信号)来做决定，
had to still pool language and make decisions.

319
00:18:21,440 --> 00:18:23,030
就像你所看到的当今猿类。
You see this in apes today.

320
00:18:23,140 --> 00:18:27,000
人类也有同样的行为和信号，
And people have the same sort of behaviors and signals.

321
00:18:27,110 --> 00:18:29,800
这是我们互相之间的社会感，
It's our social sense of each other,

322
00:18:29,910 --> 00:18:32,370
像是统治、吸引、
things like dominance, attraction,

323
00:18:32,480 --> 00:18:34,240
兴趣、激动、恐惧之类的。
interest, excitement, fear.

324
00:18:34,350 --> 00:18:37,570
这些都是诚实信号的例子。
Those can be examples of honest signals.

325
00:18:37,690 --> 00:18:39,810
这些猿类并不具有像我们一样的意识，
They're not very conscious to us,

326
00:18:39,920 --> 00:18:43,610
但它们对于研究我们互相的行为很有意义。
but yet they have big impacts on how we behave with each other.

327
00:18:43,730 --> 00:18:46,020
Sandy想要研究
Sandy wanted to study

328
00:18:46,130 --> 00:18:49,350
无意发出的信号是如何
how involuntary signals are transmitted

329
00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:51,150
在一群人中传播的，
within groups of people,

330
00:18:51,270 --> 00:18:53,420
于是他制作了一个特殊的工具。
so he developed a special tool.

331
00:18:53,540 --> 00:18:56,160
称为社交测量卡。
They are called sociometric badges.

332
00:18:56,270 --> 00:18:59,830
这些社交测量卡
So, the sociometric badges that we have,

333
00:18:59,940 --> 00:19:01,700
可以测量你声音的声调
they measure your tone of voice --

334
00:19:01,810 --> 00:19:03,800
就是你如何说话，却不是你说的什么。
so how you say it, not what you say.

335
00:19:03,910 --> 00:19:05,470
它们也可以用一种加速度计
They also measure a certain of body language

336
00:19:05,580 --> 00:19:06,970
测量你的肢体语言。
using an accelerometer.

337
00:19:07,080 --> 00:19:12,110
今天Sandy邀请八位M.I.T的毕业生
Today, Sandy has asked eight M.I.T. graduate students

338
00:19:12,220 --> 00:19:14,210
来进行一项小组任务。
to perform a group task.

339
00:19:14,320 --> 00:19:16,650
这是一个建立小组的训练
So, this is a team-building exercise

340
00:19:16,760 --> 00:19:20,550
来看你们如何共同完成这项任务，
to look at how you guys actually work together to do this task.

